<TopicPage slug="subjects/music/intermediate/harmony-and-chords">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Harmony is <strong>notes sounding together, organised into journeys</strong>. Its physics:
    simple frequency ratios sound stable (consonance), complex ones create pull (dissonance).
    Its craft: stacking thirds into chords, chaining chords into progressions that create and
    resolve tension — the system that turns simultaneous sound into emotional narrative.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the consonance–tension–resolution machine" widget="equationStep" config={harmCfg} />

  <h2>Harmony across traditions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Western harmony is one option:</strong> built on functional chords since ~1600. Indian classical music develops melody and rhythm with drone instead — depth without chord change. Gamelan, maqam and Japanese systems have their own vertical logics.</li>
    <li><strong>Jazz extends the vocabulary:</strong> 7th, 9th, 11th, 13th chords add coloured tensions; substitutions replace chords with relatives. A jazz standard's chart is a roadmap the performers redesign in real time.</li>
    <li><strong>Pop harmony is efficient:</strong> four chords, endless variations — the craft is melody, production and timing more than novelty. The Axis progression works because its tension pattern is universally legible.</li>
    <li><strong>Film scoring weaponises harmony:</strong> unresolved dissonance under a quiet scene does the audience's worrying for them. Composers choose chords the way editors choose cuts.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Piston, Harmony; Huron, Sweet Anticipation (the psychology of expectation); Aldwell & Schachter.</p>
</TopicPage>
